# Release r2. Byte-for-byte the same structure as r1, re-issued with a new
# identifier — which is what most annotation releases are for any one gene. The
# digest differs, so the axis moves; nothing a rule reads differs, so nothing
# should be reported. That combination is the alert-fatigue case, and it is a
# release rather than a fabrication: RefSeq re-issues genes whose coordinates
# have not moved all the time.
##gff-version 3
# A synthetic annotation over tests/data/mini.fa.
#
# The coordinates are invented and the file says so: what these fixtures test is
# the parser, the strand handling and the coordinate arithmetic, none of which
# care whether the numbers correspond to a real locus. Validating against real
# RefSeq annotation is a separate exercise and needs the real file.
#
# PLUSG  three coding exons on the plus strand,  120 + 90 + 300 = 510 nt
# MINUSG three coding exons on the minus strand, 60 + 90 + 150  = 300 nt
# ALTG   two transcripts, neither tagged MANE Select
# DEMOG  two coding exons, 600 + 300 = 900 nt, with a poly-T run inside exon 1
#        at 3300-3309 — the case where normalisation moves a variant and the
#        moved position is still inside coding sequence
17	test	gene	500	2000	.	+	.	ID=gene-PLUSG;gene=PLUSG
17	test	mRNA	500	2000	.	+	.	ID=rna-NM_000001.1;Parent=gene-PLUSG;gene=PLUSG;transcript_id=NM_000001.1;tag=MANE Select
17	test	CDS	500	619	.	+	0	ID=cds-1;Parent=rna-NM_000001.1
17	test	CDS	900	989	.	+	0	ID=cds-2;Parent=rna-NM_000001.1
17	test	CDS	1500	1799	.	+	0	ID=cds-3;Parent=rna-NM_000001.1
11	test	gene	100	900	.	-	.	ID=gene-MINUSG;gene=MINUSG
